00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,600
 In this lecture, we're going to focus on AWS Lambda.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:05,900
 So if you look at the architecture for AWS,

3
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:08,500
 it's a completely serverless service.

4
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:10,300
 In the case of EC2 instance,

5
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:14,000
 we had seen some of these layers were managed by AWS

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
 and few of them were managed by the customer.

7
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,000
 But in case of the AWS Lambda,

8
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:21,700
 so if you see the physical layer,

9
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:26,000
 you have the host OS, hypervisor, execution environment,

10
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
 sandbox execution, Lambda runtime,

11
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:31,000
 everything is managed by AWS.

12
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,000
 And as a customer, the only thing you need is the Genia code,

13
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
 which you can either write in Python, Java,

14
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
 and supports most of the programming languages.

15
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,000
 So that's in terms of the architecture.

16
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,000
 If you look at the management, it's a fully managed AWS service.

17
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:50,000
 So AWS manages everything, including infrastructure,

18
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,000
 scaling, maintenance, you don't need to worry about anything.

19
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
 Now in terms of the pricing model for AWS Lambda,

20
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 it's pay as you use.

21
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
 So every time you create a Lambda function,

22
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
 you have to allocate some memory,

23
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,000
 and then you'll be charged based on the memory allocated.

24
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
 The execution time, let's say your Lambda function,

25
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
 runs the Genia code and logic for five minutes.

26
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
 So you'll be charged for that five minute execution time.

27
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,000
 And number Lambda invocation.

28
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,000
 Now in terms of the execution limit,

29
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 so this is important.

30
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,000
 Lambda has a maximum 15 minute execution time per invocation.

31
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 If the execution time limit is more than 15 minutes,

32
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,000
 then you need to either take a look at ECS or EC2.

33
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,000
 Okay, in terms of the scaling,

34
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
 it's automatic and dynamic based on the request volume.

35
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
 So typically for each account in a region, it's about 1000,

36
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:55,000
 but it can be increased to tens and thousands if you send a request to AWS.

37
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
 Okay, now the important thing, the use cases.

38
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,000
 So the first one is you can write the orchestration logic for your Genia.

39
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,000
 Now let's try and understand this through an example.

40
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
 So this is the most common architectural pattern for AWS Genia applications.

41
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,000
 So you have the API gateway, Lambda, and Amazon bedrock.

42
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
 So the key things to remember about AWS Lambda,

43
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:23,000
 it's an event driven serverless compute service.

44
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,000
 So what event driven means that it's not going to run by itself.

45
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
 Unless there's a trigger, there's an event.

46
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,000
 It will not run and you'll not be charged for it.

47
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,000
 Okay, so what's an event?

48
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:40,000
 Let's say our user sends a prompt,

49
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
 summarize the winter by logs.

50
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,000
 So that's an event.

51
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
 Okay, so once he sends that request through the API gateway,

52
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,000
 it will go to AWS Lambda function.

53
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,000
 And here you can write your prompt orchestration logic.

54
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,000
 So maybe you'll use some prompt engineering techniques.

55
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
 Maybe you'd send the information related to the model,

56
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
 inference parameters, system prompts, and so on.

57
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:07,000
 And then it will be sent to Amazon bedrock,

58
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,000
 and then you'll get a response.

59
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:10,000
 So that's the first use case.

60
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,000
 The second one is on a rag apps.

61
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
 So AWS has a managed service called as bedrock knowledge bases,

62
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
 and we're going to deep dive into that.

63
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
 It's another important exam topic in the next section.

64
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
 So bedrock knowledge bases provides two APIs,

65
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
 retrieve and retrieve and generate API.

66
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
 So you can write the book of three code in your Lambda function

67
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
 to retrieve the data from a rag-based apps,

68
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
 especially bedrock knowledge bases.

69
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
 Okay, so we already discussed that it can be used to write

70
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,000
 orchestration logic for journey apps.

